Helper `readAs(found, variant)`: `json` returns `found` unchanged. For other variants, `null` returns the zero value (CONVENTIONS.md §8), and anything else returns `coerce(found, "json", variant)` from `@sonobe/core`.

Parsing `path` (text, used exactly as typed; no trimming):
```
if (path === "") return { ok: true, value: object };
let recursive = false, body = path;
if (path.startsWith("..")) { recursive = true; body = path.slice(2); }
const steps = body.split("."); // any empty step ("a..b", "a.", ".a", "..") → not found
```
Resolving `steps` against a node:
```
function walk(node, steps, depth): { ok, value } {
  if (depth > 256) { warnOnce("path is nested more than 256 levels"); return { ok: false }; }
  if (steps.length === 0) return { ok: true, value: node };
  const [step, ...rest] = steps;
  if (step === "*") {
    const children = Array.isArray(node) ? node : isPlainObject(node) ? Object.values(node) : null;
    if (!children) return { ok: false };
    const out = [];
    for (const child of children) {
      const r = walk(child, rest, depth + 1);
      if (!r.ok) continue;                               // children without the rest are skipped
      if (rest.includes("*")) out.push(...(r.value as unknown[])); // several * give one flat array
      else out.push(r.value);
    }
    return { ok: true, value: out };
  }
  if (Array.isArray(node)) {
    if (!/^\d+$/.test(step)) return { ok: false };
    const i = Number(step);
    return i < node.length ? walk(node[i], rest, depth + 1) : { ok: false };
  }
  if (isPlainObject(node) && Object.hasOwn(node, step)) return walk(node[step], rest, depth + 1);
  return { ok: false };
}
```
Recursive search (`..`): with `[first, ...rest] = steps`, visit every node depth-first in document order (the node itself, then array elements by position or object values in key order). At each plain object that has own key `first`, resolve `walk(node[first], rest)` and collect successful results; recursion continues into `node[first]` as well. The result is `{ ok: true, value: collected }`, an array that may be empty. `first === "*"` is not special in recursive mode; it matches a literal `*` key.

Each frame:
```
const r = resolve(ctx.input("object"), ctx.input<string>("path"));
const variant = ctx.typeParam ?? "json";
ctx.output("value", r.ok ? readAs(r.value, variant) : zeroValue(variant));
ctx.output("found", r.ok);
```

- Steps: a step of digits indexes arrays (leading zeros allowed: "01" is 1); on objects the same step is an ordinary key ("2024"). Negative numbers are never positions. Keys are case-sensitive and own-properties only. There is no escape syntax.
- Found: true when every step resolved. A `*` step on an array or object is found even when no child matches (Value is `[]`); a `..` search is found whenever the path parses, with Value `[]` when nothing matches.
- Key order: `Object.values`/`Object.keys` order, where whole-number keys come first in ascending order, then other keys in insertion order.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values; no special case.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. A loop of paths over one object reads several values; a loop of objects with one path reads the same place in each. Longest input wins, shorter loops wrap, scalars broadcast. `*` and `..` results are JSON arrays, not loops.
- Performance: implementations may cache the parsed steps per loop index while Path is unchanged.
- Edge cases: never throws on bad paths or data; outputs the zero value and `found` false instead.
- Coming from Origami: same grammar (numbers, keys, `*`, leading `..`). Origami doesn't document empty steps, several wildcards, or depth; Sonobe treats empty steps as not found and flattens several wildcards into one array. Found is a Sonobe addition; since v153 the output type is selectable.

