Each frame:
```
const n = Math.max(2, ctx.inputCount); // number of object inputs
const out = {};
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  const o = ctx.input(`object${i}`);
  if (isPlainObject(o)) for (const k of Object.keys(o)) defineOwn(out, k, o[k]);
  else if (o !== null) warnOnce(i, `Object ${i} isn't a JSON object and was skipped`);
}
ctx.output("object", out);
```

- Precedence: last write wins across inputs in port order (Object 1 first). This matches `Object.assign({}, o1, o2, …)`, including key positions: a key's position is where it first appeared, and whole-number keys still list first.
- Depth: shallow. Values are copied by reference; nested objects are never merged.
- Non-objects: `null` (the default for an unset JSON input) is skipped silently. Arrays, text, numbers, and booleans are skipped and warn once per restart per input per loop index through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`.
- Keys: store with own-property definitions so `__proto__` becomes an ordinary key and never changes the prototype.
- Immutability: always a new object; never mutate an input.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values; no special case.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. Looped inputs zip into a loop of merged objects (longest wins, shorter loops wrap, scalars broadcast). A whole loop of objects is not folded into one object.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears the warn-once flags. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: added in v193 with no doc page; its port names aren't published. The release note says duplicate keys overwrite previous values, which Sonobe matches. Importers map unnamed inputs in order to `object1…`.

State per loop index: `{ base: undefined, items: [], output: [], warned: false }`.

Helper `toJson(v)`: a runtime Color becomes "#RRGGBBAA"; a non-finite number becomes 0; `undefined` becomes `null`; arrays and plain objects map recursively; media references stay as objects; everything else passes through.

Each frame:
```
const input = ctx.input("array");
let dirty = false;
if (state.base === undefined || (input !== state.base && !jsonEqual(input, state.base))) {
  state.base = input;   // new starting array: drop appended items
  state.items = [];
  dirty = true;
}
if (ctx.pulsed("reset") && state.items.length > 0) { state.items = []; dirty = true; }
if (ctx.pulsed("append")) {
  if (state.items.length < 10000) { state.items = [...state.items, toJson(ctx.input("item"))]; dirty = true; }
  else if (!state.warned) { state.warned = true; ctx.services.log("warn", `${ctx.id}: Array Append holds at most 10,000 appended items`); }
}
if (dirty) {
  const base = Array.isArray(state.base) ? state.base : [];
  state.output = [...base, ...state.items];
}
ctx.output("output", state.output);
```

- Pulses: `append` and `reset` are pulse inputs (`ctx.pulsed`: an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean). A held `true` appends once. Pulses on consecutive frames each append. At most one item is appended per frame per loop index.
- Precedence: re-seeding runs first, then Reset, then Append. A Reset and an Append in the same frame leave Array plus the new item.
- Re-seeding: `jsonEqual` is deep structural equality (same kind; arrays in order; objects with the same key set in any order). Because the check is by value, a feedback wire from Output back into Array through Delay One Frame does not duplicate items: the next frame's Array already contains them and the appended list restarts empty.
- Snapshot: Item is converted with `toJson` on the pulse frame and never re-read. Output reflects an append on the same frame the pulse arrives.
- Output identity: the output array changes identity only when its contents change.
- Frame 0: the first evaluation seeds `base` from Array. A pulse on frame 0 appends on frame 0.
- Loops: per-index state. A scalar Append pulse with a looped Item appends each index's own item to that index's list and outputs a loop of arrays. New indices start from Array; removed indices drop their state. To gather a loop into one array, use Loop to Array.
- Edge cases: a non-array Array (null, object, text) counts as `[]` for the base. The 10,000 cap applies to appended items; further pulses are ignored with one warning per restart per index.
- Retargeting: changing the type keeps appended items as they were stored.
- Muted: Output passes Array through (the first JSON input), and pulses don't append. State is kept.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears appended items and the warning flag on every index. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: same Array, Item, and Append ports; Origami's output is unnamed and it has no Reset input. Origami doesn't document whether items persist across input changes; Sonobe clears them when Array changes by value.

