Helpers:
- `toJson(v)`: a runtime Color becomes "#RRGGBBAA"; a non-finite number becomes 0; `undefined` becomes `null`; arrays and plain objects map recursively; everything else passes through.
- `jsonEqual(a, b)`: same kind (null, boolean, number, string, array, object); numbers with `===` (so `0 === -0`); strings exact (case-sensitive, no trimming or Unicode normalization); arrays of equal length with equal elements in order; objects with the same own key set and equal values, in any key order. Media references compare as objects.

Each frame:
```
const array = ctx.input("array");
const item = toJson(ctx.input("item"));
let index = -1;
if (Array.isArray(array)) {
  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) if (jsonEqual(array[i], item)) { index = i; break; }
}
ctx.output("index", index);
ctx.output("contains", index >= 0);
```

- Matching: the first (lowest) position wins. No type coercion between kinds: text "3" never matches number 3, and `true` never matches 1.
- Non-arrays: objects, text, and `null` give −1 and false, without a warning.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values; no special case.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. A looped Item over one array gives a loop of positions and booleans, one per row. Longest input wins, shorter loops wrap, scalars broadcast.
- Edge cases: `index` is an index output that may be −1; downstream `index` inputs clamp it to 0, so check Contains before using it as a position.
- Muted: outputs −1 and false, as if nothing matched.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's patch is named Index Of, with the same Array, Item, Index, and Contains ports. Origami doesn't document how values compare; Sonobe uses exact JSON equality.

