Helper `toJson(v)`: a runtime Color becomes "#RRGGBBAA"; a non-finite number becomes 0; `undefined` becomes `null`; arrays and plain objects map recursively; media references stay as objects; everything else passes through.

Each frame:
```
let text;
try {
  text = JSON.stringify(toJson(ctx.input("json")), null, ctx.input<boolean>("pretty") ? 2 : undefined) ?? "null";
} catch {
  text = "";
  warnOnce("JSON to Text can't write a value that contains itself");
}
ctx.output("text", text);
```

- Format: standard JSON (RFC 8259) as produced by `JSON.stringify`: no trailing commas, strings quoted and escaped, non-ASCII characters written as-is, integers without a decimal point, and very large or small numbers in exponent form (`1e+21`). Pretty uses 2-space indentation and `\n` line breaks; empty arrays and objects print as `[]` and `{}`.
- Top-level values: text prints quoted (`"hello"`), numbers and booleans print as literals, and `null` or an unset input prints `null`.
- Key order: `Object.keys` order (whole-number keys ascending first, then insertion order), so output is identical in every host.
- Cycles: only values from scripts can contain themselves; outputs `""` and warns once per restart per loop index through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`.
- Performance: implementations may cache the last input identity and Pretty per loop index and skip re-serializing.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values; no special case.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index; a loop of JSON values gives a loop of texts.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears the warn-once flag. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: renamed from Text from JSON in v207; the ports are unnamed in Origami. Origami's indentation isn't documented; Sonobe pretty-prints by default for readability. Importers leave Pretty at true.

Stateless (implementations may cache the last text and result per loop index to skip re-parsing).

Each frame:
```
let text = ctx.input<string>("text");
if (text.charCodeAt(0) === 0xfeff) text = text.slice(1); // byte-order mark
let json = null, error = false, message = "";
if (text.trim() !== "") {
  try { json = JSON.parse(text); }
  catch (e) { error = true; message = `Text isn't valid JSON: ${e.message}`; }
}
ctx.output("json", json);
ctx.output("error", error);
ctx.output("errorMessage", message);
```

- Grammar: strict RFC 8259 via `JSON.parse`. Top-level scalars are accepted (`42`, `true`, `"hi"`, `null`). Surrounding whitespace is allowed. Comments, trailing commas, single quotes, `NaN`, and NDJSON (several values on separate lines) are errors.
- Numbers: parsed as 64-bit doubles, so integers beyond 2^53 lose precision. Duplicate keys keep the last value.
- Error Message: always starts with "Text isn't valid JSON: "; the rest is the host engine's parser message and may differ between hosts, so tests should check the prefix only. No console warning, since text being typed is often invalid for a while.
- Timing: the result appears on the same frame Text changes; an invalid text gives `null` immediately rather than holding the last valid value.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values; no special case.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index; a loop of texts gives loops of results, errors, and messages.
- Muted: outputs `null`, false, and "". Error Message does not echo Text, although the default bypass would pass the text input through to it.
- Coming from Origami: added in v207 with no doc page, so its port names aren't published; Sonobe names them Text, JSON, Error, and Error Message. Error and Error Message are Sonobe additions.

State per instance: `{ url: undefined, token: 0, pending: null, json: null, loading: false, error: false, message: "" }`.

Each frame:
```
const assetId = String(ctx.node.settings?.asset ?? "");
const url = assetId === "" ? undefined : ctx.services.resolveAssetUrl(assetId);
if (state.token === 0 || url !== state.url) {
  state.url = url;
  const token = ++state.token;        // results from older requests are ignored
  state.pending = null;
  const fetch = ctx.services.platform.fetch;
  if (assetId === "") { set(state, null, false, false, ""); }
  else if (url === undefined) { set(state, null, false, true, `No asset named "${assetId}" in this project`); }
  else if (!fetch) { set(state, null, false, true, "This host can't read project files"); }
  else {
    state.loading = true;              // keep the previous json while reloading
    fetch(url)
      .then((r) => (r.ok ? r.text() : Promise.reject(new Error(`the file couldn't be read (status ${r.status})`))))
      .then((text) => { if (token === state.token) state.pending = { ok: true, text }; },
            (e) => { if (token === state.token) state.pending = { ok: false, message: String(e?.message ?? e) }; });
  }
}
if (state.pending) {
  const p = state.pending; state.pending = null; state.loading = false;
  if (!p.ok) set(state, null, false, true, `Couldn't load ${assetId}: ${p.message}`);
  else {
    const text = p.text.charCodeAt(0) === 0xfeff ? p.text.slice(1) : p.text;
    try { set(state, JSON.parse(text), false, false, ""); }
    catch (e) { set(state, null, false, true, `${assetId} isn't valid JSON: ${e.message}`); }
  }
}
if (state.loading) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("json", state.json);
ctx.output("loading", state.loading);
ctx.output("error", state.error);
ctx.output("errorMessage", state.message);
```
(`set(state, json, loading, error, message)` assigns those four fields.)

- Settings: `asset` is an asset id from `assets/assets.json` (kind `json`). Drag-and-drop and the inspector write this setting; the patch has no input ports.
- Async (CONVENTIONS.md §16.10): the read starts on the first evaluation and whenever the resolved URL changes (a replaced file gets a new content-addressed URL). Results are stored in `state` by the promise and applied on a later `evaluate`; `ctx.output` is never called from a callback. A newer request invalidates older ones through `token`.
- Frame 0: Loading is true and JSON is `null` on frame 0; data appears on the first frame after the read settles.
- Errors: a missing asset, a failed read, and invalid JSON set Error with a readable message and JSON `null`. An empty `asset` setting is idle: no error. Parser details after the fixed prefix come from the host engine; tests should check the prefix. Each distinct error message is also logged once through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`.
- Loops: no loopable inputs; one instance, scalar outputs.
- Evaluation: `alwaysEvaluate` is true because outputs change when the read finishes, without any input change.
- Platform and simulation: reads go through `ctx.services.platform.fetch` on the URL from `resolveAssetUrl`. The desktop app, the web player, and the headless host (which stubs `fetch` to read the project folder) all provide it. For reproducible traces, a deterministic host must settle asset reads before the next `step`, so data arrives on frame 1.
- Muted: outputs `null`, false, false, and "", and starts no reads.
- Restart and dispose: restart resets state and reads the file again. Dispose increments `token` so a late result is ignored; there is nothing else to release.
- Coming from Origami: Data File (`builtin.dataFile`) is undocumented and appears only inside published system components; its ports aren't published. Importers map its embedded data to a project asset and set `asset`.
